  "explanation": "The therapist’s response supports autonomy by inviting the patient to choose what to share rather than directing the conversation or imposing assumptions. “Can you tell me about what’s been going on?” is open-ended and non-coercive, which gives the patient control over the pace, depth, and focus of disclosure. This can reduce pressure and help the patient feel respected as the expert on their own experience. The wording also conveys curiosity and availability without judgment, creating a safer interpersonal climate for exploration. Clinically, this kind of invitation often strengthens engagement because it communicates that the patient’s perspective matters and that their narrative will be followed, not overridden.",
